      "language": "maxima"
    },
    "aximar": {}
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Riemannian Metrics and Geodesics [S+N]\n\nA **Riemannian metric** $g$ on a smooth manifold $M$ assigns to each point an inner product on the tangent space $T_p M$, varying smoothly with $p$. In local coordinates,\n\n$$g = g_{ij}(x)\\,dx^i \\otimes dx^j$$\n\n(symmetric, positive definite at each point). $g$ lets us measure:\n\n- **Lengths** of vectors: $\\|v\\|_g = \\sqrt{g_{ij} v^i v^j}$.\n- **Angles** between vectors via $g(u, v)$.\n- **Lengths** of curves: $L(\\gamma) = \\int \\|\\dot\\gamma\\|_g\\,dt$.\n\nA **geodesic** is a curve that locally minimises length — the manifold's analogue of a straight line. For $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with the standard metric, geodesics are straight lines. On $S^2$, they're great circles. On a general manifold, they solve the **geodesic equation**:\n\n$$\\ddot\\gamma^k + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\,\\dot\\gamma^i\\,\\dot\\gamma^j = 0$$\n\nwhere $\\Gamma^k_{ij}$ are the **Christoffel symbols** of $g$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"numerics-sundials\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Christoffel symbols [S+N]\n\nThe Christoffel symbols are determined by the metric:\n\n$$\\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\,g^{kl}\\,(\\partial_i g_{jl} + \\partial_j g_{il} - \\partial_l g_{ij}).$$\n\n(With Einstein summation; $g^{kl}$ is the inverse metric.) For the round metric on $S^2$ with coordinates $(\\theta, \\phi)$ ($\\theta$ = colatitude, $\\phi$ = longitude),\n\n$$g = d\\theta^2 + \\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\phi^2.$$\n\nCompute the Christoffel symbols symbolically:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Round metric on S²: g = dθ² + sin²θ dφ² */\ng_metric : matrix([1, 0],\n                  [0, sin(theta)^2])$\ng_inverse : invert(g_metric)$\nprint(\"g =\")$ g_metric;\nprint(\"g^{-1} =\")$ g_inverse;\n\n/* Christoffel symbols. Coordinates [θ, φ] indexed 1, 2 */\n/* Γ^k_ij = (1/2) g^{kl} (∂_i g_{jl} + ∂_j g_{il} - ∂_l g_{ij}) */\ncoords : [theta, phi]$\ngamma_christoffel(k, i, j) := (1/2) * sum(g_inverse[k, l] *\n    (diff(g_metric[j, l], coords[i])\n   + diff(g_metric[i, l], coords[j])\n   - diff(g_metric[i, j], coords[l])), l, 1, 2)$\n\nprint()$\nprint(\"Non-zero Christoffel symbols on S²:\")$\nfor i : 1 thru 2 do\n  for j : 1 thru 2 do\n    for k : 1 thru 2 do block([cs],\n      cs : ratsimp(gamma_christoffel(k, i, j)),\n      if cs # 0 then print(\"  Γ^\", k, \"_\", i, j, \"=\", cs))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 22,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "g =\nmatrix([1,0],[0,sin(theta)^2])\ng^{-1} =\nmatrix([1,0],[0,1/sin(theta)^2])\nNon-zero Christoffel symbols on S²:\n  Γ^ 2 _ 1 2 = cos(theta)/sin(theta)\n  Γ^ 2 _ 2 1 = cos(theta)/sin(theta)\n  Γ^ 1 _ 2 2 = -(cos(theta)*sin(theta))"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "On $S^2$, the non-zero Christoffel symbols are:\n- $\\Gamma^\\theta_{\\phi\\phi} = -\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$\n- $\\Gamma^\\phi_{\\theta\\phi} = \\Gamma^\\phi_{\\phi\\theta} = \\cot\\theta$.\n\nThese are the \"fictitious force\" terms that appear when you write Newton's laws in non-Cartesian coordinates.\n\n## Geodesic equation on $S^2$\n\nPlug the Christoffels into the geodesic equation:\n\n$$\\ddot\\theta = \\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\,\\dot\\phi^2,$$\n$$\\ddot\\phi = -2\\cot\\theta\\,\\dot\\theta\\,\\dot\\phi.$$\n\nSolve numerically with `np_cvode` to trace a great circle:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* State: [θ, θ̇, φ, φ̇] */\ngeo_rhs : [\n  theta_dot,\n  sin(theta) * cos(theta) * phi_dot^2,\n  phi_dot,\n  -2 * (cos(theta)/sin(theta)) * theta_dot * phi_dot\n]$\n\n/* Initial conditions: start at equator (θ = π/2), heading at 30° from equator */\ntheta0 : float(%pi)/2$\nphi0 : 0.0$\nv0 : 1.0$  /* tangent vector magnitude */\nang0 : float(%pi)/6$  /* 30° tilt */\ntheta_dot_0 : v0 * sin(ang0)$\nphi_dot_0 : v0 * cos(ang0) / sin(theta0)$  /* must scale by 1/sin θ */\n\ntspan : np_linspace(0, 2*float(%pi), 200)$\nresult : np_cvode(geo_rhs, [t, theta, theta_dot, phi, phi_dot],\n                  [theta0, theta_dot_0, phi0, phi_dot_0], tspan)$\n\nth_traj : np_to_list(np_slice(result, all, 1))$\nph_traj : np_to_list(np_slice(result, all, 3))$\n\n/* Convert to Cartesian for plotting */\nxs : map(lambda([i], sin(th_traj[i]) * cos(ph_traj[i])), makelist(j, j, 1, length(th_traj)))$\nys : map(lambda([i], sin(th_traj[i]) * sin(ph_traj[i])), makelist(j, j, 1, length(th_traj)))$\nzs : map(lambda([i], cos(th_traj[i])), makelist(j, j, 1, length(th_traj)))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 23,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Plot the geodesic on $S^2$ — should be a great circle.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* The sphere is drawn at radius 0.99 so the trajectory — which lives on the\n   unit sphere — sits just outside it and stays visible. */\ngeo_pts : makelist([xs[i], ys[i], zs[i]], i, 1, length(xs))$\n\nax_draw3d(\n  parametric_surface(0.99*sin(v)*cos(u), 0.99*sin(v)*sin(u), 0.99*cos(v),\n                     u, 0, 2*%pi, v, 0, %pi),\n  opacity=0.6, colorscale=\"Blues\",\n  color=\"crimson\", marker_size=3, name=\"geodesic γ(t)\",\n  points(geo_pts),\n  title=\"Geodesic on S² — solves the geodesic equation, traces a great circle\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"y\", zlabel=\"z\",\n  showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 24,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
