    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Distance and the metric\n\nFor two points $p, q \\in M$, the **geodesic distance** is\n\n$$d(p, q) = \\inf_\\gamma L(\\gamma)$$\n\nover all smooth paths from $p$ to $q$. This makes $M$ a metric space; for a connected manifold, $d$ defines the same topology as the manifold structure.\n\nFor $S^2$ with the round metric, geodesic distance = central angle = $\\arccos(\\langle p, q\\rangle)$ for $p, q$ on the unit sphere.\n\n## What metric structure buys\n\nWith $g$ on $M$, you can:\n\n- **Measure**: lengths, angles, areas, volumes.\n- **Move**: parallel transport (next notebook), geodesic flow.\n- **Optimise**: minimise functionals like $\\int \\|\\dot\\gamma\\|$ — calculus of variations.\n- **Decompose**: orthogonal splittings of vector fields (Helmholtz / Hodge).\n- **Quantify** how curved the space is (next notebook).\n\nRiemannian geometry is what makes general relativity possible: spacetime is a Lorentzian (signature $(-, +, +, +)$) manifold, geodesics are world-lines of free particles, and the **curvature** of $g$ encodes gravity.\n\n## What's coming\n\nThe Christoffel symbols hint at what comes next: **curvature**. Christoffels alone aren't tensors (they transform inhomogeneously under coordinate change), but they combine into a tensor — the **Riemann curvature tensor** — that encodes how parallel transport fails to commute, how geodesics deviate from each other, and (in 2D) how the manifold differs from being flat.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nLee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. 2nd edn. Cham: Springer International Publishing. doi:[10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9) — chs. 2 (*Riemannian Metrics*), 4–5 (connections) and 6 (*Geodesics and Distance*).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
